++    <h2>How it works</h2>
++    <p>Four five-by-five grids in a square; the plaintext pair is located in the two plain grids and read out of the opposite corners of the rectangle.</p>
++    <p>Digraph substitution across two keyed 5x5 grids. No padding and no reversible pairs, unlike Playfair.</p>
++
++    <h2>A worked example</h2>
++    <p>Plaintext:</p>
++    <pre><code>MEET ME BY THE OLD CLOCK TOWER AT DAWN</code></pre>
++    <p>Key: <code>top=EXAMPLE, bottom=KEYWORD</code></p>
++    <p>Ciphertext:</p>
++    <pre><code>NEMSNEMURBMLKKEHIWDSHXXSMMEWWG</code></pre>
++    <p class="note">This example is generated by running the cipher when the page is built,
++    and the round trip is checked, so it always matches what the solver does.</p>
++
++    <h2>How it is broken</h2>
++    <p>Fifty key cells — two Playfair grids — so a from-scratch search does not finish. With either keyword known the rest follows quickly.</p>
++
++    <h2>History and context</h2>
++    <p>Delastelle again. It fixes Playfair's two embarrassments: doubled letters need no padding, and no pair ever encrypts to itself reversed.</p>
