++    <h2>How it works</h2>
++    <p>Take every k-th letter, then every k-th starting from the next, and so on.</p>
++    <p>ct = pt[::k] + pt[1::k] + ... + pt[k-1::k]</p>
++
++    <h2>A worked example</h2>
++    <p>Plaintext:</p>
++    <pre><code>MEET ME BY THE OLD CLOCK TOWER AT DAWN</code></pre>
++    <p>Key: <code>5</code></p>
++    <p>Ciphertext:</p>
++    <pre><code>MEELOTEBOOWDEYLCEATTDKRWMHCTAN</code></pre>
++    <p class="note">This example is generated by running the cipher when the page is built,
++    and the round trip is checked, so it always matches what the solver does.</p>
++
++    <h2>How it is broken</h2>
++    <p>One small unknown. Try every stride.</p>
++
++    <h2>History and context</h2>
++    <p>The scytale of Sparta in modern clothing: a strip of leather wound round a baton of an agreed thickness, described by Plutarch.</p>
++
++    <h2>At a glance</h2>
++    <table class="facts"><tbody><tr><th>Family</th><td>Transposition ciphers</td></tr><tr><th>Key</th><td>stride</td></tr><tr><th>Keyspace</th><td>unbounded</td></tr><tr><th>Search cost</th><td>cheap</td></tr><tr><th>Minimum text</th><td>6 characters</td></tr><tr><th>Also known as</th><td>scytale, stride, caesar_box</td></tr></tbody></table>
++    <p class="note">These figures come from the solver's cipher registry when this page is
++    built, so they describe the implementation you can run above rather than an idealised
++    version of the cipher.</p>
